In SQL, HAVING and WHERE are both used to filter records, but they serve different purposes.

we can use the both in one query

🔍 1. WHERE Clause
Rows ko filter karta hai GROUP BY se pehle
Jo simple condition ho (e.g. age > 18), wo WHERE mein hoti hai
Used with individual rows.


🔍 2. HAVING Clause
Groups ko filter karta hai GROUP BY ke baad
Jab aap aggregate functions use karte ho (COUNT, SUM, AVG), tab HAVING use hota hai
Used to filter grouped rows (aggregated data).


🔁 Difference Table – Roman Urdu Mein:
| Feature   | WHERE             | HAVING                                   |
| --------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Filtering | Individual rows   | Groups (after GROUP BY)                  |
| Aggregate | Use nahi karta    | Aggregate functions ke sath use hota hai |
| Timing    | GROUP BY se pehle | GROUP BY ke baad                         |


Example:
To filter by age before grouping:

SELECT name, COUNT(*)
FROM users
WHERE age > 30
GROUP BY name;


To filter by grouped data:

SELECT name, COUNT(*)
FROM users
GROUP BY name
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

In summary, use WHERE to filter rows before grouping and HAVING to filter after aggregation.
✅ Summary (Roman Urdu):
WHERE rows ko filter karta hai (normal conditions)

HAVING groups ko filter karta hai (aggregate conditions)